<h2 id="phone">What stays on the phone</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Your position</b>, read by GPS for the map, the guidance and the alerts. Outside an outing, the app only asks for an approximate position, when you look for the nearest areas.</li>
<li><b>Your tracks, your outings and the pace of your groups</b>, recorded on the phone. Android backup is turned off for the app: they are not copied to the cloud.</li>
<li><b>Your day plans</b>, computed on the phone.</li>
<li><b>The voice</b>, produced on the phone, with no network.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="choice">What you choose to send</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>The day plan.</b> The app writes it as a text message for the person you choose, in your messaging app. It leaves only if you send it yourself. The same goes for the "back home" message.</li>
<li><b>The group.</b> If you create or join a group, during the outing your phone shares your first name and your position with the phones of the group, by Bluetooth, a few dozen metres apart. No server, no network.</li>
<li><b>Reports.</b> If you report something on the ground (ice, an obstacle, a closed trail…), the report goes to the project's server with its position and time, and a photo if you add one, without its metadata. Other skiers nearby see it, then it expires by itself, within 7 days unless confirmed. It is tied to an identifier drawn at random by the app and renewed every 30 days; the server keeps only a hash of it. No account, no free text. You can delete your report.</li>
<li><b>OpenStreetMap notes.</b> If you report a mistake on the map, the app prepares a public, anonymous note at your position for the mappers of <a href={link('osm', 'en')} rel="noopener">OpenStreetMap</a>. You read it and send it yourself.</li>
<li><b>Sharing an outing.</b> The GPX or FIT file goes only to the app you choose. With <a href={link('healthConnect', 'en')} rel="noopener">Health Connect</a>, the app writes the outing there (time, route, distance, climb) and reads only the heart rate of the hours of that outing, to put it in the file.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="server">What the server receives</h2>
<p>To download or update a pack, the phone asks the server for files, as a browser does. The server sees the file asked for, hence the area chosen. It does not receive your position and does not record your IP address; its logs keep only the path, the response, the size and the duration, for a few days. Requests go through Cloudflare, which handles them under <a href={link('cloudflarePrivacy', 'en')} rel="noopener">its own policy</a>. There is no account.</p>
<p>Apart from the reports described above, the app sends nothing else to the server.</p>

<h2 id="never">What we do not do</h2>
<ul>
<li>No ads.</li>
<li>No audience measurement or advertising tracker in the app.</li>
<li>No sale of data.</li>
<li>No public map of your off-piste routes.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="permissions">Android permissions</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Precise location</b>, including with the screen off during an outing you started, for voice guidance. A notification stays visible while guidance runs. <b>Approximate location</b>, when you look for the nearest areas.</li>
<li><b>Notifications</b>, for guidance, the reminder before the time to be back, and alerts.</li>
<li><b>Vibration</b>, for the guidance vibrations.</li>
<li><b>Nearby devices (Bluetooth)</b>, only for the group.</li>
<li><b>Camera</b>, to scan the QR code of a group or add a photo to a report.</li>
<li><b>Health Connect</b>, only when you share an outing.</li>
<li><b>Internet access</b>, to download and update packs and send your reports.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="release">Notified of the release</h2>
<p>If you leave your address in the "Get notified of the release" form at the bottom of the pages, the project's server keeps <b>your e-mail address, the language of the site (French or English) and the date</b> you signed up. Nothing else: no name, no IP address.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Why:</b> to send you a single message, when the app comes out. No newsletter, no other use, never passed on to anyone.</li>
<li><b>Where:</b> on the project's server, hosted in France, in the Paris region of <a href={link('oracleCloudRegions', 'en')} rel="noopener">Oracle Cloud</a>. Requests to it go through Cloudflare, which handles them under <a href={link('cloudflarePrivacy', 'en')} rel="noopener">its own policy</a>.</li>
<li><b>How long:</b> the whole list is deleted once that message is sent.</li>
<li><b>Unsubscribing:</b> at any time, with the link shown once you sign up or the one in the release message; the address is removed at once.</li>
<li><b>Anti-robot check:</b> the form uses <a href={link('turnstile', 'en')} rel="noopener">Cloudflare Turnstile</a>, an invisible check whose script is loaded only when you use the form. Your IP address is used, without being kept, for that check and to limit sign-ups to five per hour.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="site">This website</h2>
<p>Static pages served by <a href={link('githubPages', 'en')} rel="noopener">GitHub Pages</a>: no cookies, no analytics. A script of a few lines handles the language. On your first visit to the home page, it reads your browser's preferred language and opens the French version if that is French. When you pick FR or EN at the top of a page, it keeps that choice in your browser's local storage. A second script of a few lines opens and closes the menu on a phone and runs the pause button of the home page pictures; it stores nothing. Nothing is sent, apart from the release form if you use it (<a href="#release">above</a>).</p>
<p>GitHub keeps its own server logs, see its <a rel="noopener" href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy statement</a>.</p>

<h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
<p>For a question about your data, <a rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/tracesblanches/tracesblanches/issues">ask it on GitHub</a>. For a security problem, follow the <a rel="noopener" href="https://github.com/tracesblanches/.github/blob/main/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>.</p>
</div>
